  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, is private equity collapsing yet? Late last \nweek, the industry publicly reported its second  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "largest loss ever? Private credit markets that \nfinance all the debt field speculation have seen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive withdrawals over liquidity concerns. \nA Bang Capital European loan tunch defaulted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the first time since 2008. Investor \npayouts have slowed to a multi-deade low,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also not seen since the global financial crisis. \nPeople can't pull their money out fast enough.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The few public assets that we do have information \non are down by as much as 30%. At the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the broader market is up by the same amount, and \nthe number of unsold companies acquired by these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36800,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "firms has ballooned to over 32,000. It clearly \ndoesn't sound great, but uh at the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last year the industry supposedly had its second \nbest year ever, which kind of makes me look like a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bit of an idiot, right? Private equity firms made \nalmost a trillion dollars in new acquisitions and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps more importantly sold over $700 billion \nworth of businesses, which was only ever surpassed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by 2021 when interest rates were significantly \ncheaper. But this does raise the obvious question  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of how can both of these reports be true at the \nsame time. Were the naysayers, myself included,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just wrong about this industry, or was 2025 \nsomething of a going out of business sale?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "70% of the nation's nursing homes are now owned \nby forprofit companies. Private equity getting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into dentistry. How many losses there are so \nfar throughout the private equity and private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "credit spaces over time. If a liquidity issue \nis extended, it can turn into a credit issue.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cascade Capital Group and its affiliate Legacy \nHealthcare bought the nursing home. A group  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "led by Blackstone has agreed to take control of \nsoftware group Medallia from Tama Bravo in a deal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that will cement one of the largest wipeouts in \nthe history. Okay, so I have obviously been very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "critical of this industry in the past. Mostly \nbecause of the perverse incentives it gives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financeers to inshitify products, lay off workers \nand separate themselves from the consequences,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also partially because it's just not a good \nbusiness model. It's structured from the ground up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to slowly eat itself while paying out huge bonuses \nfor the privilege. So yeah, late last month,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Toma Bravo, a major private equity firm, publicly \nannounced the industry's second biggest loss ever.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Medallia was and still is a profitable, albeit \nboring business that makes software to track  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer feedback from online reviews and those \nlittle faces you poke when leaving a shop. You  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know how companies are always collecting your data \nfrom your smart fridge for some reason? Medallia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is one of the programs they feed that data into \nto try to sell you stuff more effectively. Tommo  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bravo acquired this business using a combination \nof their investors money and an additional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "estimated 1.8 billion in private credit loans \ncoming from other big names in the space like KKR,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164480,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apollo, and Blackstone. After the acquisition, \nthe company took on even more debt. And then when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest rates rose, it took on more debt again \nto maintain its interest payments. As a shock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to absolutely nobody, this was not sustainable. \nAnd just over a week ago, it was announced that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179360,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company would be handed over to its debtors \nand that the original private equity firm in this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whole arrangement would recognize a loss for the \nfull $5 billion it had initially invested. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weird part is that the Medallia business \nitself is still profitable. It still has a long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193360,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "list of clients, and if it wasn't for all of the \ndebt that the private equity company had piled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "onto it, it would still be making a decent amount \nof money. Tommo Bravo also wasn't short on cash.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They could have just paid off this debt and \nkept the company, but they instead just decided  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to write it off entirely and hand the keys over \nto their lenders to make it their problem. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is literally the equivalent of someone putting \n80% down on an investment property that is cash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flow positive, borrowing 20% to complete the \npurchase and then letting the bank foreclose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the house as soon as interest rates went up on \ntheir adjustable rate mortgage. It sounds dumb,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it does kind of go to show that this industry \nisn't really behaving what you might call normally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230400,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the moment. And there are three reasons \nwhy stuff like this is happening at the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time that on the surface at least business is as \ngood as ever. The first reason is that 2025 was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a little bit of a rush for the exits. On the top \nline, the year was the second biggest for private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245760,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equity ever. Exit value hit $717 billion which is \nup 47% on 2024. And that sounds very impressive.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But when you look at how that number was achieved, \nthere are some problems. Deal count actually fell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "6% to 3,18 total exits, which meant this growth \nwas achieved by pushing the average deal size to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a record $1.2 billion. And that average is being \ndragged up by a handful of absolutely enormous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transactions. Just 13 of their sales accounted for \nalmost a third of all transactions in total. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without them, the industry would indeed still \nbe shrinking. The largest of these deals was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the $55 billion Electronic Arts Take Private led \nby the Saudi public investment fund, which to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clear was a sovereign wealth fund buying a video \ngame company with the assistance of some private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equity partners. A lot of the small to middle \nmarket companies that have sort of defined this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industry like the dentist offices, the plumbing \ncompanies, the regional healthcare chains, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uh YouTube channels, those are still struggling to \nfind buyers. 2025 was also the first year interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates came down meaningfully, which allowed a \nlot of funds to borrow again to shuffle their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inventory of companies around. Again, this really \nis just someone flipping real estate investments.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317680,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if they can manage their interest payments, \nselling activity tends to pick up when interest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates are lower because sellers know that buyers \ncan borrow more to fund their purchases. The only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference is that instead of trading people's \nhomes back and forth, they are trading people's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "livelihoods. on the almighty Excel spreadsheet. \nThey are both just cash flow generating assets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are easy to get loans on. Anyway, another \nbig factor was that the regulatory environment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also softened considerably. The FTC restarted \nearly termination of merger reviews and granted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over 100 requests by mid 2025. The new chairman \nof the commission, Andrew Ferguson, publicly said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351520,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the agency must get out of the way quickly. So, \nbasically, in 2025, for the first time in 3 years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funds had cheaper money and less regulatory \nfriction. And a lot of them saw this as their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chance to offload their winners and make major \nacquisitions while the window was open. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to play devil's advocate a bit, this was probably \nthe correct call for these investment firms. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big rush for the exit did create the image \nof activity that was really just making up for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lost time. Even before the recent news, there were \nsome uh inconvenient details underneath. The one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric that was not making up for lost time was \nrealized profits. Actualized payments back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investors who, you know, theoretically want a \nreturn on their investments remained extremely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low. Distributions as a percentage of net asset \nvalue stayed flat at 14% in 2025, a level not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen since 2008 and 2009 during the actual global \nfinancial crisis. And this was back at a time when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the industry was considerably smaller overall. \nand they have now lagged historical averages for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four straight years running, which is a new record \nfor the modern PE industry. So, the industry was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reporting huge exit values while barely paying \nanyone back. Now, maybe you could argue that all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this was getting reinvested, except that the \nnumber of new funds successfully raising capital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has basically hdved over the last 4 years. If the \nindustry was really firing on all cylinders, this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably shouldn't be happening. A lot of that \nrecord- setting exit volume was also just private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equity funds trading companies to other private \nequity funds, which inflates both the acquisition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number and the exit number simultaneously. \nThis always happens a bit, but 2025 saw a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive uptick. Secondary transactions hit a \nrecord $226 billion with continuation vehicles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alone reaching $115 billion, up 53% on the prior \nyear. Now, like a lot of things in this industry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that probably needs a bit of dejargoning. Private \nequity funds are supposed to have finite lives,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually 10 to 12 years. During that time, \na private equity firm will launch the fund,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market it to investors, raise money, buy assets, \nuh, improve them, flip them for a profit, and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribute the profits out to investors. Again, \nin theory, if this goes well, the firm can usually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "launch multiple different funds all at the same \ntime each with their own vintage, which is again  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a douchy Finance Bro way of referring to the \nyear the fund was launched. So to really stretch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lingo, the PE firm is the winery and the PE \nfunds are the barrels which are again supposed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be used up within a set time period. Now that \nself-imposed time frame exists for a reason.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In theory, it forces managers to actually sell \ntheir companies and realize returns rather than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just keeping a portfolio on the books, claiming \nit's worth billions and charging a 2% management  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fee on that hypothetical number indefinitely. \nAgain, in theory, it forces them to prove what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516400,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their holdings are actually worth, which was also \na part of the reason why 2025 saw such an uptick,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because a lot of the vintages were expiring. But \nwhen there are just not that many buyers and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "self-inflicted clock is ticking, it creates some \nperverse incentives. The industry has started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reluctantly building workarounds. Continuation \nvehicles are where a fund sells a company to a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fund run by the same manager. evergreen funds that \nnever technically expire and net asset value loans  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where funds borrow against their own reported \nportfolio values to pay distributions to investors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the meantime. It's more extend and pretend that \nhas sort of been enabled by slightly lower rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and much looser oversight. And look, I'm trying \nto be more positive about things. So maybe this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is all to say that the industry wasn't exactly \ndying. It was in the process of changing, although  "
