  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use code HMW50 for 50% off your first year of \ntotal money clarity. Now, it's probably worth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repeating in all of these videos that private \nequity is an extremely broad term. All it really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means is any investment not listed on public stock \nmarkets. Typically, this means private non-listed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies, but these days, it more broadly means \nanything from non-listed real estate investments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all the way to things like music royalties and \neven video game IP. If there is something that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generates a cash flow, there will probably be a \nPE firm there to speculate on it. Normally when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people talk about private equity, particularly in \nthe context of the harm it does, they are talking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about buyout funds. These are the ones that go and \nacquire local businesses, load them up with debt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to cut costs and flip them for a profit a few \nyears later. This kind of private equity is indeed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not doing well. The number of new funds raising \nmoney in the space has effectively hedged. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711360,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have underperformed the regular stock market over \nthe last several years and they are running out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of small to medium businesses willing to sell. So \nthe actual growth in PE broadly has mostly come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from somewhere else entirely. Large funds buying \nlarge companies, often by specifically taking them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off the stock market in a process called going \nprivate, which as you might have been able to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "piece together, is the opposite of going public. \nThat $55 billion EA deal is an extreme example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the trend is much broader than that. Wealthy \nindividuals and institutions already own most of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stock market and these same groups are allowed \nto invest in private equity. In the past, I have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often repeated the statistic that the top 10% of \nhouseholds own 93% of the stock market. That is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually not true anymore. Today, it's down to \nonly about 87%. The top 1% has also fallen from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "controlling 52 to 50%. Now, on the surface, this \nmight look like a step in the right direction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making asset ownership more equitable, even if \nit is still very topheavy. In reality, what is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually happened is that these groups have just \ntransitioned into private markets that regular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investors don't have access to. If you look at all \nfinancial assets, including these gated private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "markets, it is in fact more concentrated than \never. So, for a lot of companies, the only real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785520,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reason to go public anymore is if there's enough \nretail hype around the business to get them a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better price than they could negotiate privately. \nA lot of companies have figured out it's just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easier to sell in private markets. they can \navoid the regulatory overhead of being publicly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "listed and still raise the same amount of money \nfrom the same institutional investors anyway,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means that a lot of PE funds have basically \nbecome glorified index funds filled with boring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conservative businesses that would have previously \ngone onto the S\u0026P 500 or the Russell 2000. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's probably not great for the average retail \ninvestor because it means that more of the public  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stock market is going to be filled with either \nmega cap tech companies that already have huge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "index weights or companies that are only going \npublic because they know retail hype will get them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better pricing than they could in private markets. \nPut another way, if a company could sell itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a higher price to sophisticated institutional \ninvestors who spend months doing due diligence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837360,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there should probably be a pretty good reason \nwhy they'd choose to instead sell that valuable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asset to you. Major institutional investors \nare naturally wary of stretched valuations in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mega cap tech. So if they want broad exposure to \ncompanies outside of that space, private markets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have kind of become the place to do it. And well, \neven if they do want the exposure, it's also worth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pointing out that a huge amount of recent deal \nvalue and PE has come from financing data center  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "construction or buying the land to build them \non, which helped drive up the headline numbers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but is again not really what most people picture \nwhen they hear private equity. So, if we are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being generous, my predictions of their imminent \ndemise had been greatly exaggerated outside of one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876240,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particular niche. It has instead mostly been \nreplaced with a private stock market for rich  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people as opposed to the well public stock market \nfor rich people. The traditional leverage buyout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "model is shrinking and struggling. It sounds good, \nbut it gets better. But even for the investors on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inside, there's still a growing problem. A \nlot of the money that made all of this possible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is starting to dry up. We have already made a \nvideo about it, but in brief, private credit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funds stepped into the gap left by banks after \n2008, offering riskier but higher interest rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loans to companies that couldn't get traditional \nfinancing. And for a while, that worked well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially when rates were lower. But when rates \nwent up and stayed up, a lot of these borrowers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found themselves stacking new debt on top of old \ndebt just to keep up with their interest payments.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Exactly what happened with Medallia. A lot of \nthese private credit loans were packaged into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CLLOs's, collateral loan obligations, which is \nbasically the same concept as the collateralized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debt obligations from 2008, except instead of \nhome loans underneath them, they're backed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corporate debt. Now, just a few weeks ago, a CLL \ntrunch managed by Bank Capital defaulted for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first time in Europe since the global financial \ncrisis. Now, to be fair, a junior CLLO trunch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defaulting is literally what the risk structure \nis designed for. The junior tranches absorb losses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so their senior tranches stay protected. That is \nthe whole point. But it also does sound familiar,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right? Fitch has suggested this might not stay \nisolated and that this could be the first of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small cluster rather than a one-off. On the retail \nside, investors have been trying to pull their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money out of private credit funds about as fast as \ntheir agreements will allow. In Q1 2026, five of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972160,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the six major private credit funds gated investor \nwithdrawals to effectively stop the private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalent of a run on the bank. Non-listed BDC's \nreturned about $7 billion to investors during the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarter while only raising $5 billion, marking the \nfirst time these private credit vehicles saw more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "money going out than coming in. Across the entire \nindustry in the first half of 2026, deal volume  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993200,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dropped 34% compared to 2025 with the total number \nof PE transactions down 67%. Which in my defense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means I wasn't wrong about the industry so much \nas I was wrong about the timing, which I guess is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basically the same thing. Anyway, there are two \nother external variables that have contributed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to this boom and bust. The first is that a lot \nof funding for these private markets came from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "countries in the Persian Gulf. In the past, they \nhad been using their oil revenue to make major  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acquisitions across a range of industries. But \nobviously, this year, their revenue has been cut  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off. So, this particular source of new funding has \nbeen a lot slower on top of an already shrinking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market. The second thing is that one of the \nbiggest target industries for broader private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equity has been having its own problems. Over the \npast decade, PE firms really like software as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "service businesses because they were relatively \ncapital-like and they worked well when rolled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up together with other similar companies to \nexpand their product suites. So instead of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "SAS company selling one product, they could become \na portfolio of companies selling in one solution,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mostly as big software packages targeted at other \nbusinesses. Again, to use the recent example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tommo Bravo was one of the biggest players in the \nspace and Medallia was just one of the dozens of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similar holdings they had previously acquired. But \n2026 has not been a kind year for SAS companies. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot of businesses have started to believe they \ncan now vibe code workable alternatives to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enterprise software they've been paying annual \nsubscriptions for. And even if that's not actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1076480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true for most companies yet, investors think \nit is. And that's what matters for valuations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The industry is calling it the SAS apocalypse. \nRoughly a trillion dollars in aggregate SAS market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capitalization was erased in Q1 of 2026 alone. The \nmedian public SAS company revenue multiple fell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094000,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from 6.2x at the end of 2024 to 3.3x March 2026. \nOf course, private companies don't get constantly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "updated pricing information until they are forced \nto sell or be forclosed on. Secondary market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buyers are now demanding discounts of 20% or more \nto take SAS heavy PE portfolios off managers hands  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up from around 5% just a few weeks before that. \nThis is why Tomo Bravo decided to hand Medallia  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over to its creditors. In theory, it had $5 \nbillion in equity. But after these adjustments,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was underwater on its loans, even with a very \nmodest leverage ratio. So, it just made more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense to walk away and record the loss. Bravo's \ngeneral partner, uh, Mr. Bravo himself said at a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1136640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conference shortly after, \"We were moving really \nfast during that time. We underwrote really fast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "growth, and in hindsight, we paid too much \nbecause that growth didn't materialize.\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, I know that this is just one example, but it \nis a big one and probably the first of many to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exposed pantsless as the tide goes out. According \nto an industry report from Oliver Weineman,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "80% of PE buyers now site AIdriven commoditization \nas the number one risk to SAS valuations. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other thing about all of this is that we \ngenuinely don't know how good or bad things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really are yet. The private credit industry is \nprivate. Disclosures are few and far between.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can only estimate what's actually going on \nbased on the rare moments things go so publicly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wrong that they become impossible to hide or from \nthe handful of metrics that do get reported. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be fair, private equity becoming a boring \nwalled garden of equally boring companies only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accessible to accredited investors would actually \nbe preferable to the old model of leverage buyout  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funds strip mining local businesses. But more \nbroadly, the market can stay irrational longer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1195840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than any fund can stay solvent or some dumb \nYouTuber can maintain their ego. But there is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one element to this that won't wait around. \nWe've mentioned it briefly, but go and watch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1204960,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this video next to find out how private credit \nmight bring the find out stage sooner than would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1208880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "otherwise be expected. And don't forget to like \nand subscribe to keep on learning how money works."
    } ]
  } ]
}
